1
00:00:01,150 --> 00:00:05,810
After discussing Access Control Lists and
Capabilities, we can now move to a different

2
00:00:05,810 --> 00:00:10,290
topic, that of Authentication.

3
00:00:10,290 --> 00:00:14,970
Simply put, authentication is needed to be
sure (to the extent possible) about who the

4
00:00:14,970 --> 00:00:21,640
user is before enforcing a certain policy.
This is because, as we saw, policies are user-specific.

5
00:00:21,640 --> 00:00:28,910
There are different ways for a user to prove
he/she is who he/she says: one option can

6
00:00:28,910 --> 00:00:34,670
be for the user to show that he/she knows
something (like a password),

7
00:00:34,670 --> 00:00:40,930
another to prove that he/she has something,
or to prove that he/she is someone.

8
00:00:40,930 --> 00:00:46,830
These methods can of course be used not only
in isolation but also combined.

9
00:00:46,830 --> 00:00:54,580
If we focus on passwords, which are the solution
that is commonly adopted, we can agree on

10
00:00:54,580 --> 00:01:02,120
the fact that the following are all bad ideas
(you can have a look at this by yourself).

11
00:01:02,120 --> 00:01:08,390
A prominent example of why these are all bad
ideas is the Stuxnet attack; you can search

12
00:01:08,390 --> 00:01:14,080
for it and will see how something critical
has the Iranian nuclear program has been affected

13
00:01:14,080 --> 00:01:18,370
by one of these bad ideas.

14
00:01:18,370 --> 00:01:24,290
Once agreed that storing directly the password
associated with each user is not a good idea,

15
00:01:24,290 --> 00:01:28,440
we can look at how authentication works in
Unix.

16
00:01:28,440 --> 00:01:34,270
A simple form of authentication (which is
not adopted because it is not secure enough)

17
00:01:34,270 --> 00:01:39,460
is to store the result obtained when encrypting
a fixed block of data with the user password

18
00:01:39,460 --> 00:01:44,930
rather than storing the password itself.
As we mentioned before, if we have a cryptographically

19
00:01:44,930 --> 00:01:53,670
secure one-way function f it means that from
x we can compute y = f(x) but from y it is

20
00:01:53,670 --> 00:01:58,700
infeasible to find out x (which would be the
password in this case).

21
00:01:58,700 --> 00:02:05,180
Notice that, in this case, it is nonetheless
possible to exploit dictionary attacks (simply

22
00:02:05,180 --> 00:02:11,629
put, to try all common passwords/combinations
of words until one matches the encrypted set

23
00:02:11,629 --> 00:02:16,510
of characters).
Notice that if a match is found, all users

24
00:02:16,510 --> 00:02:21,060
having the same encryption result also have
the same password.

25
00:02:21,060 --> 00:02:27,739
A variation to make Unix authentication more
secure is that of using an additional n-bit

26
00:02:27,739 --> 00:02:32,519
salt (a random prefix of n bits) for each
user password.

27
00:02:32,519 --> 00:02:36,980
Assuming that the value is indeed random and
cannot be found out by the attacker, this

28
00:02:36,980 --> 00:02:46,379
implies that each word in the dictionary should
be tested with 2^n prefixes, which for large-enough

29
00:02:46,379 --> 00:02:48,400
n values becomes infeasible

30
00:02:48,400 --> 00:02:56,790
A different alternative is to use one-time
passwords. In this case, an interesting example

31
00:02:56,790 --> 00:03:03,709
is that of one-way hash chain functions.
Supposing you have at your disposition a cryptographically

32
00:03:03,709 --> 00:03:10,190
secure one-way function (here simply called
a hash function) for which the input and the

33
00:03:10,190 --> 00:03:16,889
output have the same length, and also supposing
you want to use up to n one-time passwords,

34
00:03:16,889 --> 00:03:24,639
you can compute the first password (P1) applying
the function n times, as shown in the slide.

35
00:03:24,639 --> 00:03:33,410
P2 can be computed applying the function n–1
times and, in general, the i-th password can

36
00:03:33,410 --> 00:03:38,299
be computed applying the function over the
(i+1)-th function.

37
00:03:38,299 --> 00:03:44,819
That is, if you want to check that password
i+1 is correct, you can check that its hash

38
00:03:44,819 --> 00:03:56,379
equals password i (but remember you cannot
find password i+1 from password i).

39
00:03:56,379 --> 00:04:01,169
In this case, the server can store P0 and
value 1.

40
00:04:01,169 --> 00:04:06,349
When the user wants to connect, the server
requests password 1, and to check if it is

41
00:04:06,349 --> 00:04:10,019
correct, it hashes it and compares it with
the local one.

42
00:04:10,019 --> 00:04:15,239
If they are the same, then the server can
increase its value to 2 (it means next time

43
00:04:15,239 --> 00:04:22,069
it will ask the user to give password 2) and
update its local password value to P1.

44
00:04:22,069 --> 00:04:25,070
And this, of course, can be repeated up to
n times.

45
00:04:25,070 --> 00:04:30,900
As I mentioned before, there are several ways
in which authentication can be performed.

46
00:04:30,900 --> 00:04:35,310
An alternative to passwords is that of challenge-response
authentication.

47
00:04:35,310 --> 00:04:40,810
A simple way to implement this is to choose
as the secret to share the answer to a certain

48
00:04:40,810 --> 00:04:46,620
question, an answer that is known by the user
but not by anyone else. This of course still

49
00:04:46,620 --> 00:04:53,090
requires the server-side storing to be secure!
The user might also choose a certain algorithm

50
00:04:53,090 --> 00:04:58,099
and provide the output of such algorithm to
the input provided by the server (in this

51
00:04:58,099 --> 00:05:03,190
case the algorithm cannot be trivial, otherwise
the attacker can learn it easily).

52
00:05:03,190 --> 00:05:11,090
Finally, a way to go is also for the server
and user to share an initial password k and

53
00:05:11,090 --> 00:05:18,189
for both to compute a certain function f over
k and a value r shared by the server. If f

54
00:05:18,189 --> 00:05:23,731
is a complex function (and user/server have
enough computational power to run it) and

55
00:05:23,731 --> 00:05:31,790
both k and r are large enough, it might be
infeasible for the attacker to recover k (even

56
00:05:31,790 --> 00:05:39,669
if f is known).

57
00:05:39,669 --> 00:05:44,600
Other alternatives include authentication
using physical objects such as smart cards

58
00:05:44,600 --> 00:05:49,520
and biometrics.
In the case of biometrics, an important aspect

59
00:05:49,520 --> 00:05:56,030
to account for is variability, to have enough
distance in the features between users while

60
00:05:56,030 --> 00:06:02,080
allowing some distance even for the same user
(our face does not look always the same, to

61
00:06:02,080 --> 00:06:06,629
make a simple example).
Of course, another important aspect is to

62
00:06:06,629 --> 00:06:11,770
realize many techniques can be used at the
same time.

63
00:06:11,770 --> 00:06:15,310
If we focus on cards, we can distinguish at
least three types.

64
00:06:15,310 --> 00:06:20,189
Magnetic stripe cards that contain read-only
values (the password for instance),

65
00:06:20,189 --> 00:06:24,430
chip cards that contain values that can be
updated, and

66
00:06:24,430 --> 00:06:29,740
Smart cards that also mount a small CPU and
are also capable of participating in the check

67
00:06:29,740 --> 00:06:34,460
for authentication, for instance when using
challenge-response authentication,

68
00:06:34,460 --> 00:06:39,599
or by having the cards computing a small encrypted
message (that could be helpful when buying

69
00:06:39,599 --> 00:06:44,710
something and having no connectivity) and
then giving such token to the seller, who

70
00:06:44,710 --> 00:06:47,610
can give it later on to the bank to claim
the money

